From 9d33c90b10643759a364d45565cdfc21a91c19a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 03:03:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Hibernate a Durable Object's connections, bound how many stay
 resident, and let a facet's alarm reach the node

Coworkerd v62, on top of v61's Durable Object log (0013). Three things the objects a team uses need,
all within src/workerd/server/.

A facet's alarm. The alarm scheduler of a namespace can only deliver to its root actors, so upstream
refuses setAlarm() on a facet -- and a Coworkerd object runs the tenant's class as a facet of the
Host. The time itself needs nothing new: ActorSqlite writes it into the facet's own database
(_cf_METADATA), which the shipper replicates and a rebuild restores with everything else. What was
missing is who fires it, and that can only be the node: the facet's class comes from the Worker
version the node names, and the firing has to be an event of that Worker (its CPU topped up, its
lease renewed, its lines kept). So FacetAlarmHooks, with the log on, reports every change to the
node over the shipper's channel instead of throwing, and every database reports what it holds as its
alarm when it is opened -- which is how a node that took the shard over learns, from the object's own
files, what is still due and what has already run. The per-process alarm record is rebuilt from the
object's databases and never carried over from the node that had them. Without the log the hook fails
as it did before.

A resident limit. The objects of a durable process cost memory even when they are idle, so at most
COWORKERD_DO_RESIDENT_OBJECTS of them (0: no limit) keep their Worker::Actor. Past it the least
recently used objects are evicted, a facet before its root: their hibernatable WebSockets hibernate
rather than close, so the connections stay up and the next message wakes the object on the same
storage. An object a request is running in is left alone and the next one taken instead. The count,
the evictions, the wakes and the time they took go to the node when they change (the state endpoint
reports them).

A write refused with its own code. The replicator's refusal of a commit now says what it was: the
object is past its own size limit (DO_OBJECT_TOO_LARGE) or the tenant's storage quota is exhausted
(QUOTA_EXCEEDED), not only that the storage is unavailable, and a database reports its size when it
is closed so a small object that never checkpoints still has one. Only the write is refused; nothing
that reads is.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
